---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const layers = `/* declared once, first, in every dist file: */
@layer bo-reset, bo-tokens, bo-primitives, bo-components, bo-utilities;

/* YOUR css — written outside any layer — always wins: */
.bo-btn { border-radius: 0; }   /* beats the component layer, no !important */`;

// GENERATED from the shipped z-index tokens — names, values, and the
// stacking rationale are parsed from tokens/z-index.css at build, never
// hand-typed (this page previously had NO stacking section at all: the
// framework's only statement of the z-index scale was a code comment,
// which is how a page-level sticky bar shipped a bare `z-index: 2` with
// nothing to check it against — roadmap 108, 2026-08-22).
const zIndexFiles = import.meta.glob('../../../../../packages/core/src/css/tokens/z-index.css', {
  query: '?raw',
  import: 'default',
  eager: true,
});
const zIndexFile = Object.values(zIndexFiles)[0];
const Z_TOKENS = [...zIndexFile.matchAll(/--(bo-z-[\w-]+):\s*(\d+);/g)]
  .map(([, name, value]) => ({ name, value: Number(value) }))
  .sort((a, b) => a.value - b.value);
---
<Gallery title="The cascade contract">
  <p class="demo-note">
    Every framework rule lives inside a named <code>@layer</code>. Unlayered CSS
    beats all layered CSS regardless of specificity — so <strong>your stylesheet is
    the escape hatch</strong>, by design, forever. No specificity wars, no
    <code>!important</code>, and leaving the framework is cheap: your overrides
    already outrank it.
  </p>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The order</h2>
    <pre><code>{layers}</code></pre>
    <ul class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>reset → tokens → primitives → components → utilities</strong>: utilities win over components (the one Tailwind-ism kept — a small curated set, not a system).</li>
      <li>The layer-order statement is byte-identical in every dist file, and re-declaring an existing order is a no-op — any à-la-carte import combination yields the same cascade.</li>
      <li>Re-branding = remapping the semantic token tier in your unlayered file; see <a href={base + '/concepts/theming'}>Theming</a>. Never override component classes for brand.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Stacking (<code>z-index</code>) — a different mechanism, don't conflate them</h2>
    <p><code>@layer</code> above decides which RULE wins when two selectors
    both match the same element — it never affects paint order between
    DIFFERENT elements. <code>z-index</code> decides what's drawn on top of
    what — it has no opinion on which rule wrote a property. A page can get
    the cascade layer completely right and still show one element behind
    another it should be in front of, because that's the other system
    entirely — precisely what happened building
    <a href={base + '/patterns/object-page'}>the object page pattern</a>:
    its own sticky title bar (page-level chrome) was losing a stacking
    fight to an ordinary component nested inside it, a table's sticky
    column header, because the page reached for an arbitrary
    <code>z-index: 2</code> instead of the framework's actual scale.</p>
    <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Token</th><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {Z_TOKENS.map((t) => (
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">--{t.name}</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">{t.value}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>The rule</strong>: every
    <code>position: sticky</code> or <code>position: fixed</code> element
    that isn't a plain in-flow box reaches for a token from this table —
    never a bare number. If none fits, that's a sign a new tier belongs
    here, not that this element gets to invent one. <strong>Where a
    PATTERN's own page-level sticky chrome collides with a component it
    contains</strong> (the object-page shape above): the page-level chrome
    wins, because it is the screen's own navigation, one level above
    anything nested in it — <code>--bo-z-sticky-page</code> exists for
    exactly that tier, sitting above every component-internal sticky value
    it might collide with, below <code>--bo-z-dropdown</code> (a dropdown
    opened from inside the page must still float above the page's own
    chrome) and <code>--bo-z-toast</code> (always wins, globally).</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>What this buys at screen #200</h2>
    <p>Overrides stay one-liners instead of specificity ladders; framework upgrades
    can't silently outrank app CSS (layered can never beat unlayered); and audits are
    mechanical — anything inside <code>@layer bo-*</code> is ours, anything outside
    is yours.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">One sharp edge: an unlayered <em>reset you didn't
    write</em> (Tailwind v3 preflight, normalize) wins by the same rule and strips
    components — wrap it in a lower layer; recipe + live proof in
    <a href={base + '/getting-started/troubleshooting'}>Troubleshooting</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <Related links={[["/concepts/theming", "Theming"], ["/concepts/tokens", "Design tokens"], ["/getting-started/troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting (reset interop)"], ["/patterns/object-page", "Object page (the stacking collision this rule fixed)"]]} />
</Gallery>
